# COCO TREE SERVICE — MOTION DESIGN RULES

## 1. PURPOSE

This rule defines the motion-design architecture and implementation standards for the entire Coco Tree Service website.

Motion is part of the product experience.

Animations must:

- reinforce the visual narrative;
- establish hierarchy;
- guide user attention;
- communicate transitions between states;
- create depth;
- simulate cinematic camera movement;
- improve perceived quality;
- remain performant;
- remain responsive;
- work with temporary placeholder assets;
- remain maintainable as the project grows.

Motion must never exist only because an effect looks impressive.

Every animation must have a visual or functional purpose.

---

# 2. CORE MOTION STACK

The project uses the following motion architecture.

## 2.1 Lenis

Lenis is responsible for smooth scrolling.

Lenis controls:

- smooth wheel scrolling;
- touch-compatible scrolling behavior where appropriate;
- scroll interpolation;
- scroll velocity information;
- synchronization with GSAP.

Lenis is the scrolling layer.

Do NOT implement a second smooth-scroll system.

Do NOT combine Lenis with another smooth-scroll library.

---

# 2.2 GSAP

GSAP is the primary animation engine.

Use GSAP for:

- timelines;
- transforms;
- opacity;
- scale;
- translation;
- rotation;
- clip-path;
- image movement;
- text animation;
- sequencing;
- state transitions;
- complex coordinated animations.

GSAP should be preferred over manually managing animation state with React.

---

# 2.3 GSAP ScrollTrigger

ScrollTrigger is the primary scroll-animation system.

Use ScrollTrigger for:

- scroll reveal;
- parallax;
- pinned sections;
- horizontal galleries;
- scroll-controlled transformations;
- progress-driven animation;
- hero camera simulation;
- section transitions;
- scroll-linked text animation;
- image scaling;
- depth effects.

Do NOT create scroll listeners manually when ScrollTrigger can solve the problem.

Avoid:

window.addEventListener("scroll", ...)

for animation purposes.

---

# 2.4 React

React controls:

- component structure;
- content;
- state;
- interaction states;
- lifecycle.

React should NOT continuously control frame-by-frame animation.

Avoid patterns such as:

setState() on every scroll frame.

Avoid using React state for:

- mouse coordinates;
- scroll progress;
- animation progress;
- continuously changing transform values.

Use GSAP refs and GSAP state instead.

---

# 2.5 CSS

CSS is responsible for:

- static layout;
- responsive layout;
- typography;
- colors;
- base transitions;
- visual fallback states;
- accessibility states.

CSS transitions may be used for simple microinteractions.

GSAP should be used for coordinated or timeline-based motion.

---

# 3. GLOBAL MOTION PRINCIPLES

## 3.1 Motion hierarchy

Motion intensity follows the hierarchy of the website.

Hero:
HIGH

Services:
MEDIUM/HIGH

Process:
MEDIUM

Gallery:
MEDIUM

FAQ:
LOW

CTA:
LOW/MEDIUM

Footer:
VERY LOW

The entire website must NOT animate with the same intensity.

---

# 3.2 Motion must have hierarchy

Primary elements receive stronger motion.

Secondary elements receive subtle motion.

Decorative elements receive minimal motion.

Never animate every element simultaneously.

---

# 3.3 Motion must feel physical

Prefer:

- smooth acceleration;
- smooth deceleration;
- controlled overshoot;
- depth;
- inertia;
- scale;
- parallax;
- masking;
- sequencing.

Avoid:

- excessive bouncing;
- random rotations;
- aggressive elastic effects;
- constant floating;
- unnecessary shaking.

---

# 4. TRANSFORM-FIRST RULE

For performance-sensitive animations, prefer:

- transform;
- opacity;
- clip-path when necessary.

Preferred properties:

transform
opacity
clip-path

Avoid animating continuously:

- width;
- height;
- top;
- left;
- margin;
- padding;
- box-shadow;
- filters.

Layout-affecting properties may trigger unnecessary reflow.

When possible:

BAD:

top: 100px

GOOD:

transform: translateY(100px)

---

# 5. CAMERA SIMULATION

Coco Tree Service uses a cinematic visual language.

Real 3D camera systems are NOT required for the Hero unless explicitly approved later.

The primary camera simulation is created using:

- scale;
- translate;
- parallax;
- layered depth;
- opacity;
- masking.

## 5.1 Scale-based camera movement

The Hero should simulate camera movement primarily through scale.

Example conceptual behavior:

Initial state:

scale: 1

During scroll:

scale: 1.08 → 1.15 → 1.22

This creates the perception that the camera is moving forward.

The exact values must be tuned visually.

Do NOT automatically use extreme scaling.

Avoid excessive zoom that causes visible image degradation.

---

# 5.2 Camera depth layers

Hero imagery should be treated as multiple depth layers when possible.

Example:

Layer 1:
background forest

Layer 2:
midground trees

Layer 3:
foreground vegetation

Layer 4:
text/UI

Different layers receive different movement values.

Example:

background:
scale + small translate

midground:
slightly larger translate

foreground:
stronger translate

content:
minimal movement

This creates simulated depth without requiring WebGL.

---

# 5.3 Camera movement direction

The default Hero movement is:

scroll down
→ camera moves forward
→ environment opens
→ visual activity becomes more visible
→ next section is revealed.

Avoid arbitrary movement that contradicts the narrative.

---

# 6. HERO MOTION SYSTEM

The Hero is the most sophisticated animated section.

The Hero must support:

- smooth scrolling;
- cinematic camera simulation;
- parallax;
- layered depth;
- text reveal;
- CTA reveal;
- scroll indicator;
- masking;
- section transition.

---

## 6.1 Hero initial state

Initial visual state:

- background visible;
- text visible or partially revealed;
- CTA visible;
- navigation visible;
- scroll indicator visible.

Initial animation must be lightweight.

Do not create a long blocking entrance animation.

---

# 6.2 Hero entrance timeline

Use a GSAP timeline.

Suggested order:

1. background opacity;
2. background scale;
3. title reveal;
4. subtitle reveal;
5. CTA reveal;
6. scroll indicator.

The sequence must feel cinematic but fast enough that the user can interact immediately.

Target entrance duration:

approximately 1.0–2.0 seconds.

Do not block interaction during the animation.

---

# 6.3 Hero scroll timeline

ScrollTrigger controls the Hero transformation.

Conceptual sequence:

SCROLL START

↓

camera scale increases

↓

background moves

↓

foreground layers move faster

↓

title transitions

↓

visual mask/reveal begins

↓

Hero environment changes

↓

next section becomes visible

SCROLL END

---

# 6.4 Hero camera values

Use GSAP transforms.

Example baseline:

background:
scale: 1 → 1.12

midground:
yPercent: 0 → -8

foreground:
yPercent: 0 → -14

content:
yPercent: 0 → -5

These values are starting points only.

Tune based on actual assets.

---

# 6.5 Placeholder compatibility

The Hero MUST work before final assets exist.

During development use:

- gradient backgrounds;
- CSS blocks;
- temporary images;
- generated placeholders;
- neutral background layers.

Animations must target DOM elements/classes/refs rather than specific image dimensions.

Do NOT hard-code animation values based on:

- final image width;
- final image height;
- specific photograph composition;
- exact focal point.

The animation system must survive replacing:

placeholder → real image

without requiring architectural changes.

---

# 7. SCROLL REVEAL SYSTEM

Scroll reveal is used throughout the website.

The reusable reveal system should support:

- fade;
- translateY;
- translateX;
- scale;
- clip-path.

Default reveal:

opacity:
0 → 1

transform:
translateY(30px) → translateY(0)

Suggested duration:

0.6–1.0 seconds.

Suggested easing:

power2.out

or

power3.out

Avoid overly elastic easing for normal content.

---

# 7.1 Reveal stagger

For groups of elements:

title
subtitle
cards
buttons

use stagger.

Example:

stagger: 0.08–0.15

Do not use large stagger values that make the page feel slow.

---

# 7.2 Reveal triggers

Elements should normally animate when entering the viewport.

Default ScrollTrigger concept:

start:
"top 85%"

end:
"top 50%"

Exact values may be adjusted per component.

---

# 8. PARALLAX SYSTEM

Parallax creates depth.

Use only when the visual composition supports it.

Do not add parallax to every image.

Preferred implementation:

GSAP + ScrollTrigger.

Use:

yPercent

or

transform: translateY()

rather than continuously updating layout properties.

---

# 8.1 Parallax intensity

Recommended hierarchy:

Background:
LOW

Midground:
MEDIUM

Foreground:
HIGH

Text:
LOW

UI:
VERY LOW

Parallax must remain subtle enough that content remains readable.

---

# 9. SCALE SYSTEM

Scale is a core motion primitive in this project.

Use scale for:

- simulated camera movement;
- image emphasis;
- card hover;
- CTA interaction;
- reveal;
- depth;
- transitions.

Avoid excessive scaling.

Typical ranges:

Content reveal:
0.96 → 1

Image reveal:
1.05 → 1

Camera:
1 → 1.10/1.20 approximately

Hover:
1 → 1.02/1.05

Exact values depend on context.

---

# 10. IMAGE MOTION

Images must feel alive without distracting from their content.

Possible image animations:

- scale;
- translate;
- clip-path;
- opacity;
- parallax.

Do NOT constantly animate images with loops unless explicitly required.

---

# 10.1 Services images

Service cards may use:

- subtle scale on hover;
- image translation;
- overlay transition;
- text movement;
- icon movement.

Example:

hover:

image:
scale 1 → 1.05

overlay:
opacity changes

title:
translateY slightly

icon:
translateX slightly

The entire card should feel like one coordinated interaction.

---

# 11. BEFORE / AFTER

Before & After interactions must prioritize usability.

Possible implementations:

- drag comparison slider;
- hover reveal on desktop;
- click/tap interaction on mobile.

The interaction must remain understandable without animation.

Animation should reinforce the comparison, not hide it.

---

# 12. HORIZONTAL GALLERY

If the Gallery uses horizontal scrolling:

GSAP ScrollTrigger controls:

- pinning;
- horizontal translation;
- progress.

Concept:

vertical scroll
→ section pins
→ gallery moves horizontally
→ section unpins
→ normal scroll resumes.

Do not implement this using manual wheel event manipulation.

---

# 13. TEXT ANIMATION

Text animations should be intentional.

Supported patterns:

- word reveal;
- character reveal;
- line reveal;
- opacity;
- translate;
- clip-path.

Default preference:

WORD / LINE REVEAL

before

CHARACTER REVEAL.

Character animations are visually expensive and should be reserved for important headlines.

---

# 13.1 Hero typography

Hero H1 may use:

- line reveal;
- word reveal;
- masking;
- vertical translation;
- opacity.

Avoid excessive character-by-character animation.

The headline must remain readable immediately after the animation.

---

# 14. MASK REVEALS

Masking is an important part of the visual identity.

Use:

clip-path

or GSAP-compatible masking techniques.

Potential uses:

- Hero transition;
- image reveal;
- section transition;
- text reveal;
- gallery entrance.

Avoid excessive masking because it can increase rendering complexity.

---

# 15. SECTION TRANSITIONS

Sections should not always simply appear one after another.

Important transitions may use:

- color transition;
- image reveal;
- clip-path;
- scale;
- overlap;
- parallax.

However, transitions must preserve navigation clarity.

The user should always understand where one section ends and another begins.

---

# 16. MICROINTERACTIONS

Microinteractions are used for feedback.

Examples:

- button hover;
- arrow movement;
- icon animation;
- card hover;
- accordion;
- cursor interaction.

Microinteractions should normally be:

FAST

approximately:

150–400ms

They should not delay the user's action.

---

# 16.1 Primary CTA

Primary CTA hover:

- slight scale;
- background transition;
- arrow movement.

Example:

button:
scale 1 → 1.02

arrow:
translateX 0 → 4px

Do not use large scale values.

---

# 16.2 Magnetic buttons

Magnetic buttons are optional.

If implemented:

- use pointer position;
- calculate relative distance from button center;
- apply small transform;
- reset smoothly on pointer leave.

Maximum movement should remain small.

Recommended maximum:

approximately 6–12px.

Do not make the button follow the cursor excessively.

Disable or simplify on touch devices.

---

# 16.3 Custom cursor

A custom cursor is optional.

If implemented:

- desktop only;
- hidden on touch devices;
- no interference with native click behavior;
- use transform;
- avoid React state updates every frame.

The cursor must never reduce usability.

If performance suffers, remove it.

---

# 17. FAQ MOTION

FAQ should use simple accordion animation.

Preferred:

height/clip animation coordinated by GSAP or CSS.

Do not use large cinematic animations here.

The information architecture has priority over visual effects.

---

# 18. REDUCED MOTION

Accessibility is mandatory.

Respect:

prefers-reduced-motion

When reduced motion is enabled:

- disable large parallax;
- disable camera zoom;
- disable decorative motion;
- minimize transitions;
- remove custom cursor;
- remove excessive stagger;
- preserve content visibility.

The website must remain fully usable.

---

# 19. RESPONSIVE MOTION

Motion must be responsive.

Desktop and mobile do NOT need identical animation.

---

## Desktop

May use:

- parallax;
- layered camera movement;
- hover;
- magnetic buttons;
- horizontal gallery;
- custom cursor.

---

## Tablet

Reduce:

- parallax intensity;
- number of animated layers;
- hover-dependent interactions.

---

## Mobile

Prefer:

- scroll reveal;
- scale;
- simple parallax;
- opacity;
- touch interactions.

Avoid:

- custom cursor;
- magnetic buttons;
- hover-only interactions;
- excessive layered movement;
- large pinned sections when they damage usability.

---

# 20. TOUCH DEVICES

Never depend exclusively on hover.

Every hover interaction must have an alternative behavior.

Example:

Desktop:
hover → image scale

Mobile:
tap/scroll → image state

---

# 21. PERFORMANCE RULES

Performance is a first-class requirement.

Prioritize:

transform
opacity

Use:

will-change

only when justified.

Do not apply:

will-change: transform

to hundreds of elements permanently.

---

# 21.1 Animation cleanup

Every GSAP animation created by a React component must be cleaned up.

Use:

gsap.context()

or an equivalent cleanup mechanism.

ScrollTriggers must be reverted when the component unmounts.

Do not leave orphaned:

- timelines;
- ScrollTriggers;
- event listeners;
- animation loops.

---

# 21.2 DOM references

Prefer:

useRef()

for animated elements.

Do not query the entire document unnecessarily.

Avoid excessive:

document.querySelectorAll()

inside components.

Scope animations to their component.

---

# 21.3 React Server Components

Components that use:

- GSAP;
- ScrollTrigger;
- Lenis;
- window;
- document;
- pointer events;

must be Client Components where required.

Use:

"use client"

only when necessary.

Do not convert the entire application into Client Components.

Keep static sections server-rendered whenever possible.

---

# 22. ANIMATION ARCHITECTURE

Animation logic must remain separate from visual markup when complexity justifies it.

Project structure:

animations/
├── heroAnimations.ts
├── scrollAnimations.ts
├── parallax.ts
└── reveal.ts

---

# 22.1 heroAnimations.ts

Responsible for:

- Hero entrance;
- Hero camera simulation;
- Hero scale;
- Hero parallax;
- Hero masking;
- Hero timeline.

It must NOT contain:

- JSX;
- business data;
- content;
- API logic.

---

# 22.2 scrollAnimations.ts

Responsible for:

- generic ScrollTrigger configurations;
- section-level scroll behavior;
- shared scroll utilities.

---

# 22.3 parallax.ts

Responsible for reusable parallax behavior.

The API should allow:

- target;
- intensity;
- trigger;
- start;
- end.

---

# 22.4 reveal.ts

Responsible for reusable reveal behavior.

It should support:

- fade;
- translate;
- scale;
- clip-path;
- stagger.

---

# 23. GSAP CONTEXT RULE

Every React animation should be scoped.

Preferred conceptual pattern:

useGSAP(() => {
    // animation
}, {
    scope: containerRef
});

or:

const ctx = gsap.context(() => {
    // animation
}, containerRef);

return () => ctx.revert();

Use the project's installed GSAP React integration when available.

---

# 24. LENIS + GSAP SYNCHRONIZATION

Lenis and GSAP ScrollTrigger must be synchronized.

The architecture must ensure:

Lenis scroll
↓
GSAP ticker / ScrollTrigger
↓
animation progress

Do NOT create independent animation clocks.

Do NOT run competing requestAnimationFrame loops.

There must be one coherent scroll-animation pipeline.

---

# 25. LOADING AND ASSET INDEPENDENCE

The motion system must not assume final assets are available.

During initial development:

Hero can use:

- CSS gradient;
- placeholder image;
- placeholder video;
- solid background.

Services can use:

- placeholder cards;
- generated temporary images;
- colored blocks.

Gallery can use:

- placeholder images.

Animations must still be implemented.

---

# 25.1 Asset replacement rule

When real assets are added later:

placeholder asset
→
real asset

must NOT require rewriting the animation architecture.

Only:

- asset path;
- dimensions/aspect ratio;
- focal positioning;
- optional animation tuning

should change.

---

# 25.2 Image aspect ratio

Components must define predictable containers.

Use:

aspect-ratio

or fixed responsive containers.

Do not allow image loading to unexpectedly change layout.

Use appropriate:

object-fit

and:

object-position.

---

# 26. VIDEO MOTION

Hero video may be introduced later.

The animation architecture must support:

- autoplay where permitted;
- muted playback;
- loop;
- poster fallback;
- desktop/mobile sources if necessary;
- lazy loading when appropriate.

Scroll-controlled video is optional and must only be implemented if the visual narrative justifies it.

If scroll-controlled video is used:

scroll progress
→
video.currentTime

must be controlled efficiently.

Do not continuously force React re-renders.

---

# 27. HERO VIDEO FALLBACK

The Hero must work if video fails.

Fallback hierarchy:

1. Hero video
2. Hero poster image
3. CSS background

The animation system must not break if the video is unavailable.

---

# 28. MOTION TOKENS

Use centralized motion constants when values are reused.

Example conceptual structure:

MOTION_DURATION
MOTION_EASE
MOTION_DISTANCE
MOTION_SCALE
MOTION_PARALLAX

Do not scatter arbitrary animation values across dozens of components.

---

# 29. EASING

Default easing:

power2.out

Use:

power3.out

for stronger cinematic movement.

Use:

power4.out

sparingly.

Avoid:

elastic

bounce

back

unless the interaction specifically requires it.

Cinematic motion should feel controlled.

---

# 30. TIMING GUIDELINES

Microinteraction:

150–400ms

UI transition:

300–600ms

Content reveal:

600–1000ms

Hero entrance:

1000–2000ms

Large cinematic transition:

1000–2000ms+

Longer durations require clear narrative justification.

---

# 31. STAGGER GUIDELINES

Small UI groups:

0.05–0.10s

Content groups:

0.08–0.15s

Large cinematic sequences:

0.10–0.25s

Avoid excessive stagger.

---

# 32. SCROLLTRIGGER GUIDELINES

Default:

scrub:
true

may be used for continuous scroll-driven animation.

For discrete reveals:

toggleActions

or:

once: true

may be preferred.

Do not use scrub for every animation.

---

# 33. PINNING

Pinned sections are allowed for:

- Hero;
- horizontal Gallery;
- major narrative transitions.

Do not pin ordinary content unnecessarily.

Pinned sections must be tested extensively on:

- desktop;
- tablet;
- iOS Safari;
- Android browsers.

---

# 34. Z-INDEX AND LAYERING

Animated layered scenes must use a predictable hierarchy.

Example:

background
z-index: 0

midground
z-index: 1

foreground
z-index: 2

content
z-index: 10

navigation
z-index: 100

Do not create arbitrary z-index values throughout the application.

---

# 35. OVERFLOW

Sections containing:

- parallax;
- scale;
- masks;
- translated images;

may require:

overflow: hidden

but this must be applied intentionally.

Do not globally apply overflow hidden to the page.

---

# 36. SCROLL PROGRESS

A global or section-specific scroll progress indicator may use:

scaleX(0 → 1)

rather than width changes.

Preferred:

transform: scaleX()

with:

transform-origin: left

This avoids unnecessary layout changes.

---

# 37. DEBUGGING

During development, animations must be easy to inspect.

Use GSAP markers when debugging:

markers: true

but remove or disable them before production.

Animation code should be organized so individual timelines can be isolated and tested.

---

# 38. ANIMATION NAMING

Use descriptive names.

Good:

heroCameraTimeline

servicesReveal

galleryHorizontalTimeline

ctaReveal

Bad:

anim1

timeline2

effect

thing

---

# 39. NO MAGIC NUMBERS

Avoid unexplained values such as:

123
47
-83
1.173

If a value has visual meaning, centralize it or document why it exists.

Example:

const HERO_CAMERA_ZOOM = 1.12;

This is preferable to:

scale: 1.12

appearing repeatedly without context.

---

# 40. MOTION COMPONENT RESPONSIBILITY

Each component owns its own local animation when possible.

Example:

Hero
→ Hero animation

ServiceCard
→ hover animation

Process
→ process reveal

Gallery
→ gallery animation

Global animation utilities should only provide reusable mechanisms.

Do not create one enormous animation file controlling the entire website unless the animation genuinely requires a global timeline.

---

# 41. GLOBAL VS LOCAL ANIMATION

Global:

- Lenis;
- ScrollTrigger configuration;
- shared motion tokens;
- shared reveal functions.

Local:

- Hero camera;
- service hover;
- process reveal;
- gallery movement;
- CTA interaction.

---

# 42. MOTION AND CONTENT

Animation must never compensate for weak content.

The sequence is:

content hierarchy
→
visual hierarchy
→
motion

not:

motion
→
content.

---

# 43. MOTION AND CONVERSION

Motion must support conversion.

Primary conversion elements:

- Get a Free Estimate;
- Call;
- Contact;
- Service selection.

CTA animations must make the action noticeable without becoming distracting.

Do not hide conversion elements behind long animations.

---

# 44. MOTION FAILURE RULE

If an animation:

- causes jank;
- delays interaction;
- harms accessibility;
- creates layout instability;
- makes mobile navigation difficult;
- consumes excessive CPU/GPU;

simplify or remove it.

Visual sophistication is secondary to usability and performance.

---

# 45. IMPLEMENTATION ORDER

Motion must be implemented in this order.

## Phase 1 — Foundation

Implement:

- Lenis;
- GSAP;
- ScrollTrigger;
- global motion configuration;
- cleanup strategy.

## Phase 2 — Hero

Implement:

- entrance timeline;
- scale camera;
- layered parallax;
- text reveal;
- CTA reveal;
- scroll indicator;
- Hero transition.

## Phase 3 — Content Reveals

Implement:

- Introduction reveal;
- Services reveal;
- Process reveal;
- Gallery reveal;
- FAQ reveal;
- CTA reveal.

## Phase 4 — Interactions

Implement:

- buttons;
- service cards;
- accordion;
- gallery;
- before/after.

## Phase 5 — Advanced Motion

Only after the fundamentals are stable:

- horizontal gallery;
- complex masks;
- magnetic buttons;
- custom cursor;
- scroll-controlled video;
- advanced camera transitions.

Do not implement every advanced effect simultaneously.

---

# 46. DEVELOPMENT WITHOUT FINAL ASSETS

This is mandatory for the first production phase.

The website must be built with temporary assets first.

The developer must NOT stop animation development because final images are unavailable.

Use placeholders with realistic:

- aspect ratios;
- dimensions;
- positioning;
- layer structure.

The objective is to validate:

- layout;
- animation;
- timing;
- responsiveness;
- component architecture;
- performance.

Final photography and video will be inserted later.

After inserting final assets, perform a visual tuning pass.

---

# 47. FINAL ASSET INTEGRATION

When real assets are introduced, evaluate:

- focal point;
- crop;
- object-position;
- contrast;
- animation intensity;
- scale limits;
- parallax intensity;
- readability;
- loading performance.

Do NOT redesign the animation architecture simply because an image changed.

Only tune animation parameters when necessary.

---

# 48. PRODUCTION QUALITY CHECKLIST

Before considering a motion system complete:

[ ] Lenis works correctly.

[ ] GSAP and ScrollTrigger are synchronized.

[ ] Hero camera simulation works.

[ ] Hero uses scale-based camera movement.

[ ] Hero parallax works.

[ ] Hero entrance works.

[ ] Text reveal works.

[ ] CTA animation works.

[ ] Section reveals work.

[ ] Services hover works.

[ ] Gallery interaction works.

[ ] FAQ animation works.

[ ] Reduced motion works.

[ ] Mobile motion is simplified.

[ ] No hover-only critical functionality exists.

[ ] GSAP contexts are cleaned up.

[ ] ScrollTriggers are reverted correctly.

[ ] No unnecessary React re-renders occur during animation.

[ ] No unnecessary scroll event listeners exist.

[ ] No excessive will-change usage exists.

[ ] No layout-jank is introduced.

[ ] Placeholder assets work.

[ ] Final assets can replace placeholders without architectural changes.

[ ] Animation values are centralized where appropriate.

[ ] Debug markers are removed before production.

[ ] Motion does not interfere with conversion.

[ ] Motion does not interfere with accessibility.

[ ] Motion does not compromise mobile usability.

---

# 49. FINAL DESIGN PRINCIPLE

The motion language of Coco Tree Service should feel:

CINEMATIC

NATURAL

CONTROLLED

PRECISE

PROFESSIONAL

TECHNICAL

The user should feel as if the website is guiding a camera through a story rather than simply displaying animated UI elements.

The primary visual metaphor is:

NATURE
→
DISCOVERY
→
INTERVENTION
→
TRANSFORMATION
→
RESULT

Motion should reinforce this sequence.

Never add animation merely because it is technically possible.

Every animation must answer:

"What does this movement communicate?"

If the answer is nothing meaningful:

DO NOT IMPLEMENT IT.
